# Dialog

`Pathogen::Dialog` is a named modal for a focused task. It uses native `<dialog>` and
`showModal()` for the top layer, background inertness, and keyboard focus containment.
Pathogen supplies the title, close control, scrolling layout, and focus/lifecycle integration.

Use a dialog when the user must finish or dismiss a task before returning to the page.
Prefer an ordinary page or disclosure for long work that does not need that interruption.

## Basic usage

```erb
<%%= render Pathogen::Dialog.new(id: "export-dialog", title: "Export samples") do |dialog| %>
  <%% dialog.with_trigger(text: "Configure export", tone: :primary, emphasis: :solid) %>

  <p>Choose the export contents for the selected samples.</p>

  <%% dialog.with_footer do %>
    <%%= render Pathogen::Button.new(
      text: "Cancel", tone: :neutral, emphasis: :outline, type: :button,
      data: { action: "click->pathogen--dialog#requestClose",
              "pathogen--dialog-reason-param": "cancel" }
    ) %>
  <%% end %>
<%% end %>
```

`with_trigger` creates a complete `Pathogen::Button`. Pass its options or a text block;
do not render another button or link inside it. Its native button type and dialog association
are component-owned. The body is the render block. `with_footer` accepts host-composed actions.
The title and built-in close control remain present without a footer or trigger.

## Options

- `title:` is required and must be nonblank. It supplies the visible heading and accessible name.
- `id:` identifies the native dialog. Supply a stable, unique ID for external triggers or Turbo replacements;
  otherwise one is generated. The heading ID is `#{id}-title`.
- `description:` is optional short plain text, associated through `aria-describedby`.
  Keep structured lists, tables, and long explanations in the body instead.
- `size:` accepts `:small`, `:medium` (default), `:large`, or `:extra_large`. Width remains viewport constrained.
- `open: true` requests modal opening when Stimulus connects. It does not emit a non-modal `open` attribute.
- `heading_level:` accepts `1..6` and defaults to `2`. Match the surrounding document structure.
- `initial_focus:` is a CSS selector inside the dialog. Its target must be visible and focusable;
  invalid, missing, or unavailable targets fall back to the title. Use an input for a short form or Cancel
  for a destructive confirmation. The title is the default for reading structured content.
- `return_focus:` is a document CSS selector used when the original opener is no longer available.
  Provide a logical, focusable fallback when a successful action removes its trigger.
- Additional HTML attributes apply to the native dialog. Use `classes:` for additional classes.
  Naming, role, modal state, IDs, targets, and trigger wiring are component-owned.

Translate caller-provided titles, descriptions, trigger labels, and action labels in the host.
The built-in close label is supplied by Pathogen's English/French locales.

## Scrolling and keyboard behavior

The body scrolls while the title/close control and footer remain visible. When the available
panel height minus header/footer height and spacing leaves less than **6rem** for the body,
the whole inner panel scrolls instead. This is a layout threshold, not a WCAG requirement.
There is one active vertical scrolling region, with a named keyboard tab stop when it overflows.

Focus enters the dialog on opening. `Tab` and `Shift+Tab` stay out of background page content;
browser chrome remains accessible. To scroll static content, Tab into the named content region
and use the browser's scrolling keys. Focus containment uses native dialog behavior.

Escape and the close/cancel controls request dismissal. Clicking the backdrop keeps the dialog open.
The close control remains available during asynchronous work. Hosts must keep an exit available;
do not use a busy state to lock dismissal.

Closing returns focus to the captured opener when it remains usable. Stacked dialogs are supported:
the topmost modal owns interaction and closing it returns focus to its opener inside the parent.
Pathogen also marks older modal elements inert while a child is active, restoring their prior
attributes afterward, to keep focus containment consistent across supported browsers.
Sibling component roots are preferable when host event listeners manage several dialogs.

## Controller and events

Register the library controllers once through `registerPathogenControllers(application)`.
The wrapper owns `pathogen--dialog`; the supplied `id` belongs to its native dialog target.
A host controller can locate it for an external trigger:

```javascript
const dialog = document.getElementById("export-dialog");
const root = dialog.closest('[data-controller~="pathogen--dialog"]');
const controller = application.getControllerForElementAndIdentifier(root, "pathogen--dialog");

controller.open({ trigger: document.getElementById("open-export") });
```

- `open({ trigger })` opens idempotently and captures the supplied opener.
- `openFromTrigger(event)` opens from the typed trigger's Stimulus action.
- `requestClose(event, { reason })` requests dismissal. The default reason is `close-button`;
  `event.params.reason` supplies a reason from a Stimulus action parameter. Escape supplies `escape`.
- `close({ restoreFocus: true })` commits a programmatic close, bypassing the dismissal guard.
  Use it after a successful host action. Lifecycle cleanup skips Pathogen’s explicit focus restoration.

The wrapper dispatches these bubbling events. Attach lifecycle listeners to that wrapper or an ancestor:

- `pathogen--dialog:opened`: `detail.trigger` is the captured opener.
- `pathogen--dialog:before-close`: cancelable; `detail.reason` identifies the request.
  A host may prevent it to offer an unsaved-changes choice, while preserving an explicit cancel/close exit.
- `pathogen--dialog:closed`: `detail.reason` identifies the completed close (`programmatic` for `close()`).

For DOM-nested dialogs, a parent listener should check `event.target` so it does not handle
a child's bubbling lifecycle event as its own. Dialog removal releases document scroll locking
and observers; Turbo cache cleanup closes the modal and releases its scroll lock without requesting
an additional focus move.
Hosts own loading content, submission, validation, authorization, and the logical focus destination
after content replacement.

## Forms and confirmations

Use a native form in the body. A footer submit button can associate with that form through `form:`;
keep Cancel `type: :button` so it does not submit or run required-field validation.
On validation failure, retain the dialog and make the errors perceivable; close after confirmed success.
Do not wrap the entire component in a form when its content includes another form.
`method="dialog"` is useful for a local completion flow: successful native submission closes
the modal directly and bypasses `before-close`, while Pathogen still synchronizes close state.
It sends no HTTP request. Use the host's normal form submission for persisted work.

For a destructive confirmation, explain the affected data and consequence, label the action specifically,
and set `initial_focus:` to Cancel. This component retains ordinary native dialog semantics;
it does not expose a separate alert-dialog variant. The host owns the actual destructive operation.

## Accessibility acceptance

Pathogen targets WCAG 2.2 A and AA, plus the design contract's enhanced target and focus policy.
Native behavior and automated checks do not establish full conformance. WCAG conformance covers
complete pages and processes; host content and integration require their own validation.

Check the Basic, Long static content, Form, Destructive confirmation, Stacked dialogs, and Accessibility
previews with keyboard, light/dark themes, forced colors, 200% text resizing, 400% zoom/320 CSS-pixel
reflow, text-spacing overrides, and a mobile software keyboard. Test reading and scrolling static
content as well as forms. Record NVDA, VoiceOver, TalkBack, and supported desktop/mobile browser
versions separately; leave combinations that have not been tested pending.

The component uses semantic surface, text, border, focus, radius, and overlay tokens. Hosts supply
translated content and compose actions from Pathogen primitives. Adoption in IRIDA Next is a separate migration.

References: [W3C native dialog technique](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/html/H102),
[APG modal dialog pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/dialog-modal/),
[WCAG conformance requirements](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#conformance-reqs),
[keyboard-accessible scrollers](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/overflow#accessibility).
